em for new melds if available.  \n2. **All Chows (2 points) + other patterns**: Since you already have two chows, aiming for all chows is plausible. You would need two more chows and a pair, all in suited tiles. Could combine with Mixed Straight (8 points) if you get chows 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in different suits, but your current chows are T6-T7-T8 and T7-T8-T9, which are overlapping and both in T suit, so Mixed Straight unlikely.  \n3. **Seek high-value patterns**: Given the exposed melds, Big Three Winds (12 points) is possible if you get three wind pungs, but you have no wind tiles in hand. Little Three Dragons (64 points) is possible if you get two dragon pungs and a pair of the third dragon, but you have no dragons.  \n4. **Quick hand with simples**: Discard honors/terminals and aim for All Simples (2 points) plus other small patterns like Pure Double Chow, Short Straight, etc. But you need 8 points total, so you'd need several small patterns.\n\nLooking at your hand:  \n- B5x2 is a pair candidate.  \n- W7x2 is also a pair candidate.  \n- T3 is isolated but could form chow with T1-T2 or T2-T4 or T4-T5, etc.  \n- B6 could form chow with B4-B5 or B5-B7.  \n- W4, W8 could form chows with W2-W3 or W5-W6 or W6-W7, etc.\n\nBut given your existing T suit chows, continuing with T suit seems most coherent. Discarding non-T tiles improves your hand toward Half Flush. Half Flush (6 points) plus some other patterns (e.g., Pure Double Chow if you get another identical chow, or Short Straight, or Self-Drawn, etc.) could reach 8 points.\n\nValid actions: Play B5, T3, B6, W4, W8, W7.  \n- **Play B5**: Discard one of your B5 pair. This breaks a potential pair, but you have another B5. B5 is a simple tile, not harming All Simples. But if you aim for Half Flush, you want to discard all B and W tiles.  \n- **Play T3**: Discard your only T suit tile. This reduces your Half Flush potential. T3 could be useful for chows (T1-T2-T3 or T2-T3-T4). But T3 is terminal, which might be harder to use.  \n- **Play B6**: Discard B6, a simple tile.  \n- **Play W4, W8, W7**: All are W suit, which you want to discard for Half Flush.\n\nReason: The most promising path is Half Flush (6 points) combined with other patterns like possibly Short Straight (1 point) if you have two consecutive chows (you already have T6-T7-T8 and T7-T8-T9, which are consecutive? They overlap, but they form a sequence of T6-T7-T8 and T7-T8-T9, which share T7-T8. This might qualify as Short Straight? Short Straight requires two chows in the same suit with successive numbers, like 234 and 567. Here you have 678 and 789, which are successive (6-7-8 and 7-8-9). Yes, that is two consecutive chows, so Short Straight (1 point) likely applicable. Also, your melds are both chows, so if you win with all chows, that's 2 points. Plus Half Flush 6 points = 8 points exactly. If you also get Self-Drawn (1 point) or other small patterns, you exceed 8. So Half Flush + All Chows + Short Straight = 6+2+1=9 points. That is a solid plan.\n\nTherefore, you should discard non-T tiles to pursue Half Flush. Among W4, W8, W7, B5, B6, which to discard? B5 is a pair, which could be useful as your final pair. Keeping a pair is important. W7 is also a pair. You have two pairs: B5x2 and W7x2. You only need one pair ultimately. For Half Flush, you cannot keep B or W pair unless it's an honor pair (honors are allowed in Half Flush). B5 and W7 are not honors, so they must be discarded. But you might temporarily keep one pair as placeholder until you get a T pair or honor pair.\n\nRight now, you have no honor tiles. So you need to draw an honor pair or a T pair. Discarding one of the pairs reduces your options but keeps the hand flexible. Since you have two pairs, discarding one tile from a pair leaves you with a single tile that might form a chow.  \n\nConsider tile availability:  \n- W4: 4 left (good supply)  \n- W7: 4 left  \n- W8: 3 left  \n- B5: 4 left  \n- B6: 3 left  \n- T3: 1 left (only 1 T3 left, since you have 1, and unshown shows 1 left total? Wait unshown: T3:1. That means only 1 T3 remains in the wall/dead. You have 1 T3, so total 1? Actually unshown counts tiles not visible to anyone. So there is 1 T3 not visible. So T3 is rare.  \n\nGiven T3 scarcity, keeping T3 might be hard to use, but discarding it might be safe since opponents might not need it.  \n\nBut for Half Flush, you want to keep T3. However, T3 is terminal, which might not be ideal for All Simples within Half Flush, but Half Flush doesn't require All Simples.  \n\nBetter to discard a W tile first to signal commitment to Half Flush. Among W4, W7, W8, W7 is a pair, so discarding one W7 breaks the pair but leaves you with one W7 that could form a chow with W5-W6 or W6-W8. But W6 is only 2 left.  \n\nAlternatively, discard W4 or W8 which are singles. Discarding a single tile is usually better than breaking a pair early. So discard W4 or W8.  \n\nBetween W4 and W8, which is safer? Looking at opponents' discards and melds:  \n- Next player: melds J3 pung and T3-T4-T5 chow, has played T2, T1, etc. No W discards recently.  \n- Across: meld B4 pung, has played W9, W1, etc.  \n- Previous: meld B6-B7-B8 chow, has played W2, W8, etc. W8 was played by previous player, so maybe W8 is somewhat safe. W4 hasn't been discarded much.  \n\nBut safety is not paramount; you need to shape your hand.  \n\nI think discard W4 first, as it's a single and not close to your other W tiles.  \n\nAfter discarding W4, you draw a tile next turn, hopefully a T or honor.  \n\nSo final choice: Play W4.  \n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
