g shape.\n\nGiven that, I should discard a tile that is less useful for forming melds.\n\nMy hand tiles: W5, W7, W7, W8, T7, B5, B6, F2.\n\nT7 is isolated, only possible chow with T5-T6 or T8-T9, but T5,T6,T8,T9 are somewhat used (see unshown: T5:2, T6:3, T8:0, T9:1). T8 is exhausted (0 left), so T7 cannot form chow with T8-T9. T5-T6-T7 possible if I get T5 and T6, but that's two tiles. So T7 is not very useful.\n\nB5-B6 can form chow with B4 or B7. B4:1 left, B7:2 left. Good chance.\n\nW5-W7-W8 need W6 to form chow. W6:1 left.\n\nF2 could be pair or pung.\n\nSo least useful might be T7. Or W5 if I focus on W7-W8 chow.\n\nI'll discard T7 to keep B5-B6 and W5-W7-W8 possibilities.\n\nSituation: Hand has two exposed T-suit chows, mixed hand with potential chows in W and B suits. Need to discard one tile to improve toward a winning hand with enough points.\nEvaluation: Discarding T7 is relatively safe (T7 is simple, no major risk) and maintains flexibility for chows in W and B suits, and keeps honor F2 for potential pung/points.\nReason: T7 is less connected given T8 exhaustion and my existing T-suit melds; keeping W5, B5, B6 offers more chow opportunities. Aim for hand with possible Mixed Straight or Half Flush later.\nAnswer: Play T7"}
