8\u201d means the **sequence** [W7,W8,W9] and you **do** have W7,W8 and the discarded tile is W9 (since W9 count 0 left but maybe it was just discarded now). The game history doesn\u2019t show the current discard\u2014it\u2019s implicit. Let\u2019s assume current discard is W9 (since W8 left is 1, not 0, so not W8).  \n\nIf current discard is W9, then Chi W8 sequence [W7,W8,W9] uses your W7,W8 and discard W9. That uses your only W8 and one W7. Then your hand becomes: W7 left, T4, B5, B6,B6 plus the new chow [W7,W8,W9] exposed. That improves hand shape: you reduce honorless simples, get a chow in W suit, but you lose flexibility for W pair.  \n\n**Reason for choice:**  \nGiven you need 8+ fan, forming another chow doesn\u2019t help much unless aiming for All Chows (2 fan) or Half Flush (6 fan) \u2014 but you have T chows and possibly W chow, still mixed suits. Currently your hand is very low fan potential. Passing keeps your hand intact, allowing you to draw and possibly shift toward a better pattern like Half Flush (only T and honors) or Full Flush (only T). But you have W and B tiles too. Actually Half Flush needs only one suit + honors; you have T chows already, but your hand has W and B tiles \u2014 so you\u2019d need to discard them. Passing now avoids committing to an exposed W chow, keeping concealed options. Since Chi W8 uses a terminal W9 and W8, it doesn\u2019t add fan except maybe Edge Wait later, but that\u2019s minimal.  \n\nGiven the very weak hand and long way to go, preserving flexibility is better. Also, W8 is a useful tile for pairing with your W7 later if you draw another W8. If you Chi, you lose that chance. So **Pass** seems better tactically.\n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
